# Line endings, stated per file type rather than globally.
#
# Every text blob here is already LF; what was missing was anything saying so.
# With core.autocrlf=true the working copy gets CRLF on checkout even though the
# stored bytes are LF, which is why `git add` printed "LF will be replaced by
# CRLF" on every file — and it is what makes byte-for-byte comparison with the
# Brewers repo impossible while the two share code.
#
# Because the blobs are already LF this costs no renormalization and produces no
# diff, which was verified rather than assumed.
#
# Deliberately structured the same way as the Brewers repo's, including the
# absence of a `* text=auto` line. That repo cannot have one — a global rule
# there has to be walked back for 458MB of Retrosheet data, and both ways of
# doing that misfire. This repo's data is small and already LF, so it could
# afford the global form; keeping the two files parallel is worth more than the
# two lines it saves, because they are heading towards being the same file.
*.js   text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf
*.css  text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.svg  text eol=lf
*.txt  text eol=lf

# Docker's files have no extension between them, so none of the rules above
# reaches either. This is not cosmetic: a CRLF Dockerfile breaks backslash line
# continuations, and the HEALTHCHECK here is written across two lines. `git add`
# printing "LF will be replaced by CRLF" is what surfaced it.
Dockerfile    text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf
# And this file, which is extensionless too and was declaring rules it did not
# apply to itself.
.gitattributes text eol=lf

# Data is generated by update-data.js and read by the parser byte-for-byte.
# These are already LF; -text rather than an eol rule so that whatever a future
# source delivers is what gets stored, which is the rule the Brewers repo needs
# and this one may as well share.
*.csv -text

# Fonts and images are binary. Saying so outright means they can never be
# corrupted by a rule added above.
*.ttf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
